WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.847
(big band jazz music)

00:00:01.847 --> 00:00:05.514
(speaking foreign language)

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.310
- [Narrator] José de la Rosa
is a third generation pachuco.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.750
Defined by their bombastic way of dress,

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.610
pachuco is a term adopted
by the Mexican-Americans

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.810
and Mexican Nationals for a subculture

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.883
dating all the way to the 1930s.

00:00:40.258 --> 00:00:43.925
(speaking foreign language)

00:01:00.488 --> 00:01:01.460
- [Narrator] And that's how the

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.540
pachuco's way of dress was born.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.680
This clothing was also a form
of protest for these men.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.970
In the face of
discrimination by Americans,

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.810
the zoot suit was a way of showing

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:13.162
that they would not change who they were.

00:01:13.162 --> 00:01:16.829
(speaking foreign language)

00:01:23.293 --> 00:01:26.460
(big band jazz music)

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.170
- [Narrator] Although the pachuco scene

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.920
originated with the Chicanos
of Southern California,

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.180
the movement thrived in Mexico as well,

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.970
thanks in part to this man, Germán Valdés,

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:06.270
better known as Tin-Tan.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:08.930
He was the Elvis of pachucos.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:10.740
Not only did he help bring pachucos

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.700
into mainstream culture in Mexico,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.570
he brought them to the
country's dance halls

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:16.833
where most pachucos can
be found to this day.

00:02:18.651 --> 00:02:20.674
(Latin jazz music)

00:02:20.674 --> 00:02:24.341
(speaking foreign language)

00:02:35.549 --> 00:02:39.049
(gentle orchestral music)

00:03:20.283 --> 00:03:22.950
